From: Trijal Saha <97483939+Trijal08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] samsung-sit: only a data context names the data subscription

Which SIM owns mobile data is a subscription-wide choice. It is not a
property of whatever context happens to be going up, and an IMS context
says nothing about it -- but SET_PREFERRED_DATA_MODEM was sent from every
setup, so on a two-SIM boot each stack claimed data for itself on the way to
its own IMS context and the two fought over it:

    stock   0x0740 x2   00, 01                  then never again
    ours    0x0740 x4   01, 00, 01, 01

Stock sends it twice, before either of its IMS contexts, and never around
them. The CP does real work for a switch, so our redundant toggle away and
back cost 2690ms on the critical path to stack 0's IMS request, against 8ms
for the same message when it did not change anything.

This was first added because an IMS context came back cause=0 and empty, no
address and no P-CSCF, and the unsent preference was the theory. That was
truncated carrier config and is fixed at its own root, so the theory is
gone and what is left is stock's rule: assert it for a context that carries
data, and leave it alone otherwise.

A bearer connect still names it as its own first step, on its own client,
and now records what it told the CP so the no-change check upstream is not
reading stale state and re-sending a switch of its own.

--- a/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-cp.c
+++ b/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-cp.c
@@ -171,6 +171,21 @@
     mm_sit_client_command (self->clients[0], request, 10, NULL, NULL, NULL);
 }
 
+/*
+ * A bearer connect names the preference as its own first step, on its own
+ * client, without coming through here. Record it so the check above is reading
+ * what the CP was actually told: left stale, the next data context would see a
+ * preference it disagreed with and send a redundant switch, which is the flap
+ * this is all here to avoid.
+ */
+void
+mm_sit_cp_note_data_stack (MMSitCp *self, guint stack)
+{
+    if (!self || stack >= 2)
+        return;
+    self->data_pref = (gint) stack;
+}
+
 static void
 prepare_complete (MMSitCp *self, const GError *error)
 {
--- a/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-cp.h
+++ b/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-cp.h
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 void mm_sit_cp_forget_sim_slot_status (MMSitCp *self);
 
 void mm_sit_cp_prefer_data_stack (MMSitCp *self, guint stack);
+void mm_sit_cp_note_data_stack   (MMSitCp *self, guint stack);
 gboolean mm_sit_cp_data_reserved (MMSitCp *self, guint8 cid);
 void mm_sit_cp_commit_data (MMSitCp *self, guint8 cid);
 void mm_sit_cp_release_data (MMSitCp *self, guint8 cid);
--- a/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-client.c
+++ b/src/plugins/samsung-sit/mm-sit-client.c
@@ -3186,6 +3186,8 @@
 
     switch (ctx->step) {
     case CONNECT_STEP_PREFERRED_MODEM:
+        /* See mm_sit_cp_note_data_stack(): this is the other sender. */
+        mm_sit_cp_note_data_stack (self->priv->cp, self->priv->stack);
         return mm_sit_request_new_set_preferred_modem (mm_sit_client_next_token (control_client (self)), self->priv->stack);
     case CONNECT_STEP_DATA_PROFILE:
         return mm_sit_request_new_set_data_profile (mm_sit_client_next_token (self), ctx->apn, ctx->pdp, ctx->apn_types, ctx->bearers, &ctx->auth, error);
@@ -3809,18 +3811,26 @@
     }
 
     /*
-     * The CP has to know which stack owns the data subscription before it is
-     * asked to create a context. Captured on hardware: with the preference
-     * still unsent, this call was answered cause=0 and empty -- no assigned
-     * address, no P-CSCF -- where the stock stack's answer to a byte-identical
-     * request carries both. The stock stack re-asserts the preference seconds
-     * before its own IMS context, which is what this mirrors.
+     * Name the stack that owns the data subscription -- but only for a context
+     * that carries data. Which SIM mobile data belongs to is a subscription-wide
+     * choice; it is not a property of whatever context happens to be going up,
+     * and an IMS context says nothing about it.
      *
-     * A bearer connect names it as its own first step, so this is only for the
-     * paths that do not go through one. A no-op when the CP already knows.
+     * Asserting it from every setup made the two stacks fight over it. Each
+     * one claimed data for itself on the way to its own IMS context, so a
+     * two-SIM boot sent 0x0740 four times -- 01, 00, 01, 01 -- where stock
+     * sends it twice, 00 then 01, and never again around either of its IMS
+     * contexts. The CP does real work for a switch, and the redundant toggle
+     * away and back cost 2690ms on the critical path to stack 0's IMS request
+     * while stack 1's reply to the same message took 8ms.
      *
+     * This was first added because an IMS context came back cause=0 and empty,
+     * with no address and no P-CSCF, and the unsent preference was the theory.
+     * That turned out to be truncated carrier config and is fixed at its own
+     * root, so the theory this mirrored is gone; what remains is the real rule,
+     * which is stock's. A bearer connect still names it as its own first step.
      */
-    if (self->priv->cp)
+    if (self->priv->cp && (apn_types & MM_SIT_APN_TYPE_DEFAULT))
         mm_sit_cp_prefer_data_stack (self->priv->cp, self->priv->stack);
 
     ctx = g_new0 (SetupContext, 1);
